  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nZonal to southwest flow aloft this afternoon into Thursday will \ncontinue to stream moisture over the Continental Divide. As a \nresult, isolated to scattered rain/snow showers will be persistent\nprimarily along the Northern Continental Divide and Rocky \nMountain Front this afternoon into Thursday morning. A cold front\ncurrently along the Hi-Line pushes south across the state today \ninto Thursday, bringing cooler temperatures to the region for \nThursday and Friday. The front also brings another slight chance \nfor patchy fog to develop across North-Central MT tonight into \nThursday and Friday morning.\n\nA digging trough from British Columbia/Pacific Northwest Coast \nincreases southwest flow aloft, bringing warm air advection and \nmoisture Friday. Precipitation chances increases throughout \nFriday across Southwest MT. However, better chances for widespread\nprecipitation across the state arrives Saturday into Sunday with \na low pressure system, with precipitation lingering into Monday. \nSo far, the main message with this system is minor snow at pass \nlevel with more moderate accumulations at the higher mountain \npeaks, and rain/snow at lower elevations (light snow trending \nmore late Sunday into Monday).\n\nBehind that system, ensembles are hinting at a ridge building in for \nthe middle of next week. This trends drier conditions with \ntemperatures building back to near or above normals, with some \nuncertainty in how much of a warm up there is.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFog Tonight:\n\nLow-level subsidence behind this cold front passage can allow for \npatchy fog development across North-Central MT later tonight into\nThursday morning. There's a 30-60% chance for fog to develop \nacross North-Central MT, isolated 10-30% chances for visibility to\ndrop to a half mile or lower with fog. One limiting factor is a \nlow stratus deck prohibiting fog development. The potential for \nfog development extends into Friday morning as well with higher- \nres and medium-res models showing low level moisture lingering. \nDepending on how deep that low level moisture is, there is a very \nlow chance for light freezing drizzle to develop along the Rocky \nMountain Front (10% chance).\n\nSnow Along Northern Rocky's Through Thursday:\n\nNot much has changed in terms of the forecast and impacts of the \nsnow along Marias Pass and the Rocky Mountain Front foothills. Minor \nsnow accumulations of 2-4\" is expected along Marias Pass, with \nlittle accumulations towards the Rocky Mountain Front. Weak low \nlevel frontogenesis along the cold front can bring moderate snow \nrates along the mountains this evening.\n\nWeekend Storm System:\n\nThere remains some uncertainty with the weekend storm system as \nthe low track will play into snow accumulations. Warm air \nadvection Friday into Saturday may make snow accumulations \nstruggle along mountain passes/higher Southwest MT valleys, \nkeeping the bulk of the snow at the higher peaks. Models have been\ntrending colder air moving in Sunday on the back edge of the low \npressure system, where there will be better chances for minor snow\naccumulations at mountain passes. There's a 20-60% chance for 3\" \nof snow at pass level in Southwest MT. Light snow looks to mix \ndown to lower elevations Sunday/Monday morning at times but \naccumulations look to be light to minimal at this time. -Wilson",
